window.TC_TAO={"legge":[{"n":1,"b":[["p","The Tao that can be trodden is not the enduring and unchanging Tao. The name that can be named is not the enduring and unchanging name."],["p","(Conceived of as) having no name, it is the Originator of heaven and earth; (conceived of as) having a name, it is the Mother of all things."],["v",["Always without desire we must be found,","If its deep mystery we would sound;","But if desire always within us be,","Its outer fringe is all that we shall see."]],["p","Under these two aspects, it is really the same; but as development takes place, it receives the different names. Together we call them the Mystery. Where the Mystery is the deepest is the gate of all that is subtle and wonderful."]]},{"n":2,"b":[["p","All in the world know the beauty of the beautiful, and in doing this they have (the idea of) what ugliness is; they all know the skill of the skilful, and in doing this they have (the idea of) what the want of skill is."],["p","So it is that existence and nonexistence give birth the one to (the idea of) the other; that difficulty and ease produce the one (the idea of) the other; that length and shortness fashion out the one the figure of the other; that (the ideas of) height and lowness arise from the contrast of the one with the other; that the musical notes and tones become harmonious through the relation of one with another; and that being before and behind give the idea of one following another."],["p","Therefore the sage manages affairs without doing anything, and conveys his instructions without the use of speech."],["p","All things spring up, and there is not one which declines to show itself; they grow, and there is no claim made for their ownership; they go through their processes, and there is no expectation (of a reward for the results). The work is accomplished, and there is no resting in it (as an achievement)."],["v",["The work is done, but how no one can see;","’Tis this that makes the power not cease to be."]]]},{"n":3,"b":[["p","Not to value and employ men of superior ability is the way to keep the people from rivalry among themselves; not to prize articles which are difficult to procure is the way to keep them from becoming thieves; not to show them what is likely to excite their desires is the way to keep their minds from disorder."],["p","Therefore the sage, in the exercise of his government, empties their minds, fills their bellies, weakens their wills, and strengthens their bones."],["p","He constantly (tries to) keep them without knowledge and without desire, and where there are those who have knowledge, to keep them from presuming to act (on it). When there is this abstinence from action, good order is universal."]]},{"n":4,"b":[["p","The Tao is (like) the emptiness of a vessel; and in our employment of it we must be on our guard against all fullness. How deep and unfathomable it is, as if it were the Honoured Ancestor of all things!"],["p","We should blunt our sharp points, and unravel the complications of things; we should attemper our brightness, and bring ourselves into agreement with the obscurity of others. How pure and still the Tao is, as if it would ever so continue!"],["p","I do not know whose son it is. It might appear to have been before God."]]},{"n":5,"b":[["p","Heaven and earth do not act from (the impulse of) any wish to be benevolent; they deal with all things as the dogs of grass are dealt with. The sages do not act from (any wish to be) benevolent; they deal with the people as the dogs of grass are dealt with."],["p","May not the space between heaven and earth be compared to a bellows?"],["v",["’Tis emptied, yet it loses not its power;","’Tis moved again, and sends forth air the more.","Much speech to swift exhaustion lead we see;","Your inner being guard, and keep it free."]]]},{"n":6,"b":[["v",["The valley spirit dies not, aye the same;","The female mystery thus do we name.","Its gate, from which at first they issued forth,","Is called the root from which grew heaven and earth.","Long and unbroken does its power remain,","Used gently, and without the touch of pain."]]]},{"n":7,"b":[["p","Heaven is long-enduring and earth continues long. The reason why heaven and earth are able to endure and continue thus long is because they do not live of, or for, themselves. This is how they are able to continue and endure."],["p","Therefore the sage puts his own person last, and yet it is found in the foremost place; he treats his person as if it were foreign to him, and yet that person is preserved. Is it not because he has no personal and private ends, that therefore such ends are realised?"]]},{"n":8,"b":[["p","The highest excellence is like (that of) water. The excellence of water appears in its benefiting all things, and in its occupying, without striving (to the contrary), the low place which all men dislike. Hence (its way) is near to (that of) the Tao."],["p","The excellence of a residence is in (the suitability of) the place; that of the mind is in abysmal stillness; that of associations is in their being with the virtuous; that of government is in its securing good order; that of (the conduct of) affairs is in its ability; and that of (the initiation of) any movement is in its timeliness."],["p","And when (one with the highest excellence) does not wrangle (about his low position), no one finds fault with him."]]},{"n":9,"b":[["p","It is better to leave a vessel unfilled, than to attempt to carry it when it is full. If you keep feeling a point that has been sharpened, the point cannot long preserve its sharpness."],["p","When gold and jade fill the hall, their possessor cannot keep them safe. When wealth and honours lead to arrogancy, this brings its evil on itself. When the work is done, and one’s name is becoming distinguished, to withdraw into obscurity is the way of Heaven."]]},{"n":10,"b":[["p","When the intelligent and animal souls are held together in one embrace, they can be kept from separating. When one gives undivided attention to the (vital) breath, and brings it to the utmost degree of pliancy, he can become as a (tender) babe. When he has cleansed away the most mysterious sights (of his imagination), he can become without a flaw."],["p","In loving the people and ruling the state, cannot he proceed without any (purpose of) action? In the opening and shutting of his gates of heaven, cannot he do so as a female bird? While his intelligence reaches in every direction, cannot he (appear to) be without knowledge?"],["p","(The Tao) produces (all things) and nourishes them; it produces them and does not claim them as its own; it does all, and yet does not boast of it; it presides over all, and yet does not control them. This is what is called “The mysterious Quality” (of the Tao)."]]},{"n":11,"b":[["p","The thirty spokes unite in the one nave; but it is on the empty space (for the axle), that the use of the wheel depends. Clay is fashioned into vessels; but it is on their empty hollowness, that their use depends. The door and windows are cut out (from the walls) to form an apartment; but it is on the empty space (within), that its use depends. Therefore, what has a (positive) existence serves for profitable adaptation, and what has not that for (actual) usefulness."]]},{"n":12,"b":[["v",["Colour’s five hues from th’ eyes their sight will take;","Music’s five notes the ears as deaf can make;","The flavours five deprive the mouth of taste;","The chariot course, and the wild hunting waste","Make mad the mind; and objects rare and strange,","Sought for, men’s conduct will to evil change."]],["p","Therefore the sage seeks to satisfy (the craving of) the belly, and not the (insatiable longing of the) eyes. He puts from him the latter, and prefers to seek the former."]]},{"n":13,"b":[["p","Favour and disgrace would seem equally to be feared; honour and great calamity, to be regarded as personal conditions (of the same kind)."],["p","What is meant by speaking thus of favour and disgrace? Disgrace is being in a low position (after the enjoyment of favour). The getting that (favour) leads to the apprehension (of losing it), and the losing it leads to the fear of (still greater calamity):—this is what is meant by saying that favour and disgrace would seem equally to be feared."],["p","And what is meant by saying that honour and great calamity are to be (similarly) regarded as personal conditions? What makes me liable to great calamity is my having the body (which I call myself); if I had not the body, what great calamity could come to me?"],["p","Therefore he who would administer the kingdom, honouring it as he honours his own person, may be employed to govern it, and he who would administer it with the love which he bears to his own person may be entrusted with it."]]},{"n":14,"b":[["p","We look at it, and we do not see it, and we name it “the Equable.” We listen to it, and we do not hear it, and we name it “the Inaudible.” We try to grasp it, and do not get hold of it, and we name it “the Subtle.” With these three qualities, it cannot be made the subject of description; and hence we blend them together and obtain The One."],["p","Its upper part is not bright, and its lower part is not obscure. Ceaseless in its action, it yet cannot be named, and then it again returns and becomes nothing. This is called the Form of the Formless, and the Semblance of the Invisible; this is called the Fleeting and Indeterminable."],["p","We meet it and do not see its Front; we follow it, and do not see its Back. When we can lay hold of the Tao of old to direct the things of the present day, and are able to know it as it was of old in the beginning, this is called (unwinding) the clue of Tao."]]},{"n":15,"b":[["p","The skilful masters (of the Tao) in old times, with a subtle and exquisite penetration, comprehended its mysteries, and were deep (also) so as to elude men’s knowledge. As they were thus beyond men’s knowledge, I will make an effort to describe of what sort they appeared to be."],["p","Shrinking looked they like those who wade through a stream in winter; irresolute like those who are afraid of all around them; grave like a guest (in awe of his host); evanescent like ice that is melting away; unpretentious like wood that has not been fashioned into anything; vacant like a valley, and dull like muddy water."],["p","Who can (make) the muddy water (clear)? Let it be still, and it will gradually become clear. Who can secure the condition of rest? Let movement go on, and the condition of rest will gradually arise."],["p","They who preserve this method of the Tao do not wish to be full (of themselves). It is through their not being full of themselves that they can afford to seem worn and not appear new and complete."]]},{"n":16,"b":[["p","The (state of) vacancy should be brought to the utmost degree, and that of stillness guarded with unwearying vigour. All things alike go through their processes of activity, and (then) we see them return (to their original state). When things (in the vegetable world) have displayed their luxuriant growth, we see each of them return to its root. This returning to their root is what we call the state of stillness; and that stillness may be called a reporting that they have fulfilled their appointed end."],["p","The report of that fulfilment is the regular, unchanging rule. To know that unchanging rule is to be intelligent; not to know it leads to wild movements and evil issues. The knowledge of that unchanging rule produces a (grand) capacity and forbearance, and that capacity and forbearance lead to a community (of feeling with all things). From this community of feeling comes a kingliness of character; and he who is king-like goes on to be heaven-like. In that likeness to heaven he possesses the Tao. Possessed of the Tao, he endures long; and to the end of his bodily life, is exempt from all danger of decay."]]},{"n":17,"b":[["p","In the highest antiquity, (the people) did not know that there were (their rulers). In the next age they loved them and praised them. In the next they feared them; in the next they despised them. Thus it was that when faith (in the Tao) was deficient (in the rulers) a want of faith in them ensued (in the people)."],["p","How irresolute did those (earliest rulers) appear, showing (by their reticence) the importance which they set upon their words! Their work was done and their undertakings were successful, while the people all said, “We are as we are, of ourselves!”"]]},{"n":18,"b":[["p","When the Great Tao (Way or Method) ceased to be observed, benevolence and righteousness came into vogue. (Then) appeared wisdom and shrewdness, and there ensued great hypocrisy."],["p","When harmony no longer prevailed throughout the six kinships, filial sons found their manifestation; when the states and clans fell into disorder, loyal ministers appeared."]]},{"n":19,"b":[["p","If we could renounce our sageness and discard our wisdom, it would be better for the people a hundredfold. If we could renounce our benevolence and discard our righteousness, the people would again become filial and kindly. If we could renounce our artful contrivances and discard our (scheming for) gain, there would be no thieves nor robbers."],["v",["Those three methods (of government)","Thought olden ways in elegance did fail","And made these names their want of worth to veil;","But simple views, and courses plain and true","Would selfish ends and many lusts eschew."]]]},{"n":20,"b":[["v",["When we renounce learning we have no troubles.","The (ready) “yes,” and (flattering) “yea;”—","Small is the difference they display.","But mark their issues, good and ill;—","What space the gulf between shall fill?"]],["p","What all men fear is indeed to be feared; but how wide and without end is the range of questions (asking to be discussed)!"],["p","The multitude of men look satisfied and pleased; as if enjoying a full banquet, as if mounted on a tower in spring. I alone seem listless and still, my desires having as yet given no indication of their presence. I am like an infant which has not yet smiled. I look dejected and forlorn, as if I had no home to go to. The multitude of men all have enough and to spare. I alone seem to have lost everything. My mind is that of a stupid man; I am in a state of chaos."],["p","Ordinary men look bright and intelligent, while I alone seem to be benighted. They look full of discrimination, while I alone am dull and confused. I seem to be carried about as on the sea, drifting as if I had nowhere to rest. All men have their spheres of action, while I alone seem dull and incapable, like a rude borderer. (Thus) I alone am different from other men, but I value the nursing-mother (the Tao)."]]},{"n":21,"b":[["v",["The grandest forms of active force","From Tao","Who can of Tao","Our sight it flies, our touch as well.","Eluding sight, eluding touch,","The forms of things all in it crouch;","Eluding touch, eluding sight,","There are their semblances, all right.","Profound it is, dark and obscure;","Things’ essences all there endure.","Those essences the truth enfold","Of what, when seen, shall then be told.","Now it is so; ’twas so of old.","Its name—what passes not away;","So, in their beautiful array,","Things form and never know decay."]],["p","How know I that it is so with all the beauties of existing things? By this (nature of the Tao)."]]},{"n":22,"b":[["p","The partial becomes complete; the crooked, straight; the empty, full; the worn out, new. He whose (desires) are few gets them; he whose (desires) are many goes astray."],["p","Therefore the sage holds in his embrace the one thing (of humility), and manifests it to all the world. He is free from self-display, and therefore he shines; from self-assertion, and therefore he is distinguished; from self-boasting, and therefore his merit is acknowledged; from self-complacency, and therefore he acquires superiority. It is because he is thus free from striving that therefore no one in the world is able to strive with him."],["p","That saying of the ancients that “the partial becomes complete” was not vainly spoken:—all real completion is comprehended under it."]]},{"n":23,"b":[["p","Abstaining from speech marks him who is obeying the spontaneity of his nature. A violent wind does not last for a whole morning; a sudden rain does not last for the whole day. To whom is it that these (two) things are owing? To Heaven and Earth. If Heaven and Earth cannot make such (spasmodic) actings last long, how much less can man!"],["p","Therefore when one is making the Tao his business, those who are also pursuing it, agree with him in it, and those who are making the manifestation of its course their object agree with him in that; while even those who are failing in both these things agree with him where they fail."],["p","Hence, those with whom he agrees as to the Tao have the happiness of attaining to it; those with whom he agrees as to its manifestation have the happiness of attaining to it; and those with whom he agrees in their failure have also the happiness of attaining (to the Tao). (But) when there is not faith sufficient (on his part), a want of faith (in him) ensues (on the part of the others)."]]},{"n":24,"b":[["p","He who stands on his tiptoes does not stand firm; he who stretches his legs does not walk (easily). (So), he who displays himself does not shine; he who asserts his own views is not distinguished; he who vaunts himself does not find his merit acknowledged; he who is self-conceited has no superiority allowed to him. Such conditions, viewed from the standpoint of the Tao, are like remnants of food, or a tumour on the body, which all dislike. Hence those who pursue (the course) of the Tao do not adopt and allow them."]]},{"n":25,"b":[["p","There was something undefined and complete, coming into existence before Heaven and Earth. How still it was and formless, standing alone, and undergoing no change, reaching everywhere and in no danger (of being exhausted)! It may be regarded as the Mother of all things."],["p","I do not know its name, and I give it the designation of the Tao (the Way or Course). Making an effort (further) to give it a name I call it The Great."],["p","Great, it passes on (in constant flow). Passing on, it becomes remote. Having become remote, it returns. Therefore the Tao is great; Heaven is great; Earth is great; and the (sage) king is also great. In the universe there are four that are great, and the (sage) king is one of them."],["p","Man takes his law from the Earth; the Earth takes its law from Heaven; Heaven takes its law from the Tao. The law of the Tao is its being what it is."]]},{"n":26,"b":[["p","Gravity is the root of lightness; stillness, the ruler of movement."],["p","Therefore a wise prince, marching the whole day, does not go far from his baggage wagons. Although he may have brilliant prospects to look at, he quietly remains (in his proper place), indifferent to them. How should the lord of a myriad chariots carry himself lightly before the kingdom? If he do act lightly, he has lost his root (of gravity); if he proceed to active movement, he will lose his throne."]]},{"n":27,"b":[["p","The skilful traveller leaves no traces of his wheels or footsteps; the skilful speaker says nothing that can be found fault with or blamed; the skilful reckoner uses no tallies; the skilful closer needs no bolts or bars, while to open what he has shut will be impossible; the skilful binder uses no strings or knots, while to unloose what he has bound will be impossible. In the same way the sage is always skilful at saving men, and so he does not cast away any man; he is always skilful at saving things, and so he does not cast away anything. This is called “Hiding the light of his procedure.”"],["p","Therefore the man of skill is a master (to be looked up to) by him who has not the skill; and he who has not the skill is the helper of (the reputation of) him who has the skill. If the one did not honour his master, and the other did not rejoice in his helper, an (observer), though intelligent, might greatly err about them. This is called “The utmost degree of mystery.”"]]},{"n":28,"b":[["v",["Who knows his manhood’s strength,","Yet still his female feebleness maintains;","As to one channel flow the many drains,","All come to him, yea, all beneath the sky.","Thus he the constant excellence retains;","The simple child again, free from all stains.","Who knows how white attracts,","Yet always keeps himself within black’s shade,","The pattern of humility displayed,","Displayed in view of all beneath the sky;","He in the unchanging excellence arrayed,","Endless return to man’s first state has made.","Who knows how glory shines,","Yet loves disgrace, nor e’er for it is pale;","Behold his presence in a spacious vale,","To which men come from all beneath the sky.","The unchanging excellence completes its tale;","The simple infant man in him we hail."]],["p","The unwrought material, when divided and distributed, forms vessels. The sage, when employed, becomes the Head of all the Officers (of government); and in his greatest regulations he employs no violent measures."]]},{"n":29,"b":[["p","If anyone should wish to get the kingdom for himself, and to effect this by what he does, I see that he will not succeed. The kingdom is a spirit-like thing, and cannot be got by active doing. He who would so win it destroys it; he who would hold it in his grasp loses it."],["v",["The course and nature of things is such that","What was in front is now behind;","What warmed anon we freezing find.","Strength is of weakness oft the spoil;","The store in ruins mocks our toil."]],["p","Hence the sage puts away excessive effort, extravagance, and easy indulgence."]]},{"n":30,"b":[["p","He who would assist a lord of men in harmony with the Tao will not assert his mastery in the kingdom by force of arms. Such a course is sure to meet with its proper return."],["p","Wherever a host is stationed, briars and thorns spring up. In the sequence of great armies there are sure to be bad years."],["p","A skilful (commander) strikes a decisive blow, and stops. He does not dare (by continuing his operations) to assert and complete his mastery. He will strike the blow, but will be on his guard against being vain or boastful or arrogant in consequence of it. He strikes it as a matter of necessity; he strikes it, but not from a wish for mastery."],["p","When things have attained their strong maturity they become old. This may be said to be not in accordance with the Tao: and what is not in accordance with it soon comes to an end."]]},{"n":31,"b":[["p","Now arms, however beautiful, are instruments of evil omen, hateful, it may be said, to all creatures. Therefore they who have the Tao do not like to employ them."],["p","The superior man ordinarily considers the left hand the most honourable place, but in time of war the right hand. Those sharp weapons are instruments of evil omen, and not the instruments of the superior man;—he uses them only on the compulsion of necessity. Calm and repose are what he prizes; victory (by force of arms) is to him undesirable. To consider this desirable would be to delight in the slaughter of men; and he who delights in the slaughter of men cannot get his will in the kingdom."],["p","On occasions of festivity to be on the left hand is the prized position; on occasions of mourning, the right hand. The second in command of the army has his place on the left; the general commanding in chief has his on the right;—his place, that is, is assigned to him as in the rites of mourning. He who has killed multitudes of men should weep for them with the bitterest grief; and the victor in battle has his place (rightly) according to those rites."]]},{"n":32,"b":[["p","The Tao, considered as unchanging, has no name."],["p","Though in its primordial simplicity it may be small, the whole world dares not deal with (one embodying) it as a minister. If a feudal prince or the king could guard and hold it, all would spontaneously submit themselves to him."],["p","Heaven and Earth (under its guidance) unite together and send down the sweet dew, which, without the directions of men, reaches equally everywhere as of its own accord."],["p","As soon as it proceeds to action, it has a name. When it once has that name, (men) can know to rest in it. When they know to rest in it, they can be free from all risk of failure and error."],["p","The relation of the Tao to all the world is like that of the great rivers and seas to the streams from the valleys."]]},{"n":33,"b":[["p","He who knows other men is discerning; he who knows himself is intelligent. He who overcomes others is strong; he who overcomes himself is mighty. He who is satisfied with his lot is rich; he who goes on acting with energy has a (firm) will."],["p","He who does not fail in the requirements of his position, continues long; he who dies and yet does not perish, has longevity."]]},{"n":34,"b":[["p","All-pervading is the Great Tao! It may be found on the left hand and on the right."],["p","All things depend on it for their production, which it gives to them, not one refusing obedience to it. When its work is accomplished, it does not claim the name of having done it. It clothes all things as with a garment, and makes no assumption of being their lord;—it may be named in the smallest things. All things return (to their root and disappear), and do not know that it is it which presides over their doing so;—it may be named in the greatest things."],["p","Hence the sage is able (in the same way) to accomplish his great achievements. It is through his not making himself great that he can accomplish them."]]},{"n":35,"b":[["p","To him who holds in his hands the Great Image (of the invisible Tao), the whole world repairs. Men resort to him, and receive no hurt, but (find) rest, peace, and the feeling of ease."],["p","Music and dainties will make the passing guest stop (for a time). But though the Tao as it comes from the mouth, seems insipid and has no flavour, though it seems not worth being looked at or listened to, the use of it is inexhaustible."]]},{"n":36,"b":[["p","When one is about to take an inspiration, he is sure to make a (previous) expiration; when he is going to weaken another, he will first strengthen him; when he is going to overthrow another, he will first have raised him up; when he is going to despoil another, he will first have made gifts to him:—this is called “Hiding the light (of his procedure).”"],["p","The soft overcomes the hard; and the weak the strong."],["p","Fishes should not be taken from the deep; instruments for the profit of a state should not be shown to the people."]]},{"n":37,"b":[["p","The Tao in its regular course does nothing (for the sake of doing it), and so there is nothing which it does not do."],["p","If princes and kings were able to maintain it, all things would of themselves be transformed by them."],["p","If this transformation became to me an object of desire, I would express the desire by the nameless simplicity."],["v",["Simplicity without a name","Is free from all external aim.","With no desire, at rest and still,","All things go right as of their will."]]]},{"n":38,"b":[["p","(Those who) possessed in highest degree the attributes (of the Tao) did not (seek) to show them, and therefore they possessed them (in fullest measure). (Those who) possessed in a lower degree those attributes (sought how) not to lose them, and therefore they did not possess them (in fullest measure)."],["p","(Those who) possessed in the highest degree those attributes did nothing (with a purpose), and had no need to do anything. (Those who) possessed them in a lower degree were (always) doing, and had need to be so doing."],["p","(Those who) possessed the highest benevolence were (always seeking) to carry it out, and had no need to be doing so. (Those who) possessed the highest righteousness were (always seeking) to carry it out, and had need to be so doing."],["p","(Those who) possessed the highest (sense of) propriety were (always seeking) to show it, and when men did not respond to it, they bared the arm and marched up to them."],["p","Thus it was that when the Tao was lost, its attributes appeared; when its attributes were lost, benevolence appeared; when benevolence was lost, righteousness appeared; and when righteousness was lost, the proprieties appeared."],["p","Now propriety is the attenuated form of leal-heartedness and good faith, and is also the commencement of disorder; swift apprehension is (only) a flower of the Tao, and is the beginning of stupidity."],["p","Thus it is that the Great man abides by what is solid, and eschews what is flimsy; dwells with the fruit and not with the flower. It is thus that he puts away the one and makes choice of the other."]]},{"n":39,"b":[["p","The things which from of old have got the One (the Tao) are—"],["v",["Heaven which by it is bright and pure;","Earth rendered thereby firm and sure;","Spirits with powers by it supplied;","Valleys kept full throughout their void","All creatures which through it do live","Princes and kings who from it get","The model which to all they give."]],["p","All these are the results of the One (Tao)."],["v",["If heaven were not thus pure, it soon would rend;","If earth were not thus sure, ’twould break and bend;","Without these powers, the spirits soon would fail;","If not so filled, the drought would parch each vale;","Without that life, creatures would pass away;","Princes and kings, without that moral sway,","However grand and high, would all decay."]],["p","Thus it is that dignity finds its (firm) root in its (previous) meanness, and what is lofty finds its stability in the lowness (from which it rises). Hence princes and kings call themselves “Orphans,” “Men of small virtue,” and as “Carriages without a nave.” Is not this an acknowledgement that in their considering themselves mean they see the foundation of their dignity? So it is that in the enumeration of the different parts of a carriage we do not come on what makes it answer the ends of a carriage. They do not wish to show themselves elegant-looking as jade, but (prefer) to be coarse-looking as an (ordinary) stone."]]},{"n":40,"b":[["v",["The movement of the Tao","And weakness marks the course"]],["p","All things under heaven sprang from It as existing (and named); that existence sprang from It as nonexistent (and not named)."]]},{"n":41,"b":[["p","Scholars of the highest class, when they hear about the Tao, earnestly carry it into practice. Scholars of the middle class, when they have heard about it, seem now to keep it and now to lose it. Scholars of the lowest class, when they have heard about it, laugh greatly at it. If it were not (thus) laughed at, it would not be fit to be the Tao."],["p","Therefore the sentence-makers have thus expressed themselves:—"],["v",["“The Tao","Who progress in it makes, seems drawing back;","Its even way is like a rugged track.","Its highest virtue from the vale doth rise;","Its greatest beauty seems to offend the eyes;","And he has most whose lot the least supplies.","Its firmest virtue seems but poor and low;","Its solid truth seems change to undergo;","Its largest square doth yet no corner show","A vessel great, it is the slowest made;","Loud is its sound, but never word it said;","A semblance great, the shadow of a shade.”"]],["p","The Tao is hidden, and has no name; but it is the Tao which is skilful at imparting (to all things what they need) and making them complete."]]},{"n":42,"b":[["p","The Tao produced One; One produced Two; Two produced Three; Three produced All things. All things leave behind them the Obscurity (out of which they have come), and go forward to embrace the Brightness (into which they have emerged), while they are harmonised by the Breath of Vacancy."],["p","What men dislike is to be orphans, to have little virtue, to be as carriages without naves; and yet these are the designations which kings and princes use for themselves. So it is that some things are increased by being diminished, and others are diminished by being increased."],["p","What other men (thus) teach, I also teach. The violent and strong do not die their natural death. I will make this the basis of my teaching."]]},{"n":43,"b":[["p","The softest thing in the world dashes against and overcomes the hardest; that which has no (substantial) existence enters where there is no crevice. I know hereby what advantage belongs to doing nothing (with a purpose)."],["p","There are few in the world who attain to the teaching without words, and the advantage arising from non-action."]]},{"n":44,"b":[["v",["Of fame or life,","Of life or wealth,","Keep life and lose those other things;","Keep them and lose your life:—which brings"]],["v",["Thus we may see,","Who loves large stores"]],["v",["Who is content","Needs fear no shame.","Who knows to stop","Incurs no blame.","From danger free","Long live shall he."]]]},{"n":45,"b":[["v",["Who thinks his great achievements poor","Shall find his vigour long endure.","Of greatest fullness, deemed a void,","Exhaustion ne’er shall stem the tide.","Do thou what’s straight still crooked deem;","Thy greatest art still stupid seem,","And eloquence a stammering scream."]],["p","Constant action overcomes cold; being still overcomes heat. Purity and stillness give the correct law to all under heaven."]]},{"n":46,"b":[["p","When the Tao prevails in the world, they send back their swift horses to (draw) the dung-carts. When the Tao is disregarded in the world, the warhorses breed in the border lands."],["p","There is no guilt greater than to sanction ambition; no calamity greater than to be discontented with one’s lot; no fault greater than the wish to be getting. Therefore the sufficiency of contentment is an enduring and unchanging sufficiency."]]},{"n":47,"b":[["p","Without going outside his door, one understands (all that takes place) under the sky; without looking out from his window, one sees the Tao of Heaven. The farther that one goes out (from himself), the less he knows."],["p","Therefore the sages got their knowledge without travelling; gave their (right) names to things without seeing them; and accomplished their ends without any purpose of doing so."]]},{"n":48,"b":[["p","He who devotes himself to learning (seeks) from day to day to increase (his knowledge); he who devotes himself to the Tao (seeks) from day to day to diminish (his doing)."],["p","He diminishes it and again diminishes it, till he arrives at doing nothing (on purpose). Having arrived at this point of non-action, there is nothing which he does not do."],["p","He who gets as his own all under heaven does so by giving himself no trouble (with that end). If one take trouble (with that end), he is not equal to getting as his own all under heaven."]]},{"n":49,"b":[["p","The sage has no invariable mind of his own; he makes the mind of the people his mind."],["p","To those who are good (to me), I am good; and to those who are not good (to me), I am also good;—and thus (all) get to be good. To those who are sincere (with me), I am sincere; and to those who are not sincere (with me), I am also sincere;—and thus (all) get to be sincere."],["p","The sage has in the world an appearance of indecision, and keeps his mind in a state of indifference to all. The people all keep their eyes and ears directed to him, and he deals with them all as his children."]]},{"n":50,"b":[["p","Men come forth and live; they enter (again) and die."],["p","Of every ten three are ministers of life (to themselves); and three are ministers of death."],["p","There are also three in every ten whose aim is to live, but whose movements tend to the land (or place) of death. And for what reason? Because of their excessive endeavours to perpetuate life."],["p","But I have heard that he who is skilful in managing the life entrusted to him for a time travels on the land without having to shun rhinoceros or tiger, and enters a host without having to avoid buff coat or sharp weapon. The rhinoceros finds no place in him into which to thrust its horn, nor the tiger a place in which to fix its claws, nor the weapon a place to admit its point. And for what reason? Because there is in him no place of death."]]},{"n":51,"b":[["p","All things are produced by the Tao, and nourished by its outflowing operation. They receive their forms according to the nature of each, and are completed according to the circumstances of their condition. Therefore all things without exception honour the Tao, and exalt its outflowing operation."],["p","This honouring of the Tao and exalting of its operation is not the result of any ordination, but always a spontaneous tribute."],["p","Thus it is that the Tao produces (all things), nourishes them, brings them to their full growth, nurses them, completes them, matures them, maintains them, and overspreads them."],["p","It produces them and makes no claim to the possession of them; it carries them through their processes and does not vaunt its ability in doing so; it brings them to maturity and exercises no control over them;—this is called its mysterious operation."]]},{"n":52,"b":[["p","(The Tao) which originated all under the sky is to be considered as the mother of them all."],["p","When the mother is found, we know what her children should be. When one knows that he is his mother’s child, and proceeds to guard (the qualities of) the mother that belong to him, to the end of his life he will be free from all peril."],["p","Let him keep his mouth closed, and shut up the portals (of his nostrils), and all his life he will be exempt from laborious exertion. Let him keep his mouth open, and (spend his breath) in the promotion of his affairs, and all his life there will be no safety for him."],["p","The perception of what is small is (the secret of) clear-sightedness; the guarding of what is soft and tender is (the secret of) strength."],["v",["Who uses well his light,","Reverting to its (source so) bright,","Will from his body ward all blight,","And hides the unchanging from men’s sight."]]]},{"n":53,"b":[["p","If I were suddenly to become known, and (put into a position to) conduct (a government) according to the Great Tao, what I should be most afraid of would be a boastful display."],["p","The great Tao (or way) is very level and easy; but people love the byways."],["p","Their court(-yards and buildings) shall be well kept, but their fields shall be ill-cultivated, and their granaries very empty. They shall wear elegant and ornamented robes, carry a sharp sword at their girdle, pamper themselves in eating and drinking, and have a superabundance of property and wealth;—such (princes) may be called robbers and boasters. This is contrary to the Tao surely!"]]},{"n":54,"b":[["v",["What (Tao’s","What his skilful arms enfold,","Sons shall bring in lengthening line,","Sacrifices to his shrine."]],["v",["Tao","And where the family it rules","The neighbourhood where it prevails","And when ’tis seen throughout the state,","Employ it the kingdom o’er,"]],["p","In this way the effect will be seen in the person, by the observation of different cases; in the family; in the neighbourhood; in the state; and in the kingdom."],["p","How do I know that this effect is sure to hold thus all under the sky? By this (method of observation)."]]},{"n":55,"b":[["p","He who has in himself abundantly the attributes (of the Tao) is like an infant. Poisonous insects will not sting him; fierce beasts will not seize him; birds of prey will not strike him."],["p","(The infant’s) bones are weak and its sinews soft, but yet its grasp is firm. It knows not yet the union of male and female, and yet its virile member may be excited;—showing the perfection of its physical essence. All day long it will cry without its throat becoming hoarse;—showing the harmony (in its constitution)."],["v",["To him by whom this harmony is known,","(The secret of) the unchanging (Tao","And in the knowledge wisdom finds its throne.","All life-increasing arts to evil turn;","Where the mind makes the vital breath to burn,","(False) is the strength, (and o’er it we should mourn.)"]],["p","When things have become strong, they (then) become old, which may be said to be contrary to the Tao. Whatever is contrary to the Tao soon ends."]]},{"n":56,"b":[["p","He who knows (the Tao) does not (care to) speak (about it); he who is (ever ready to) speak about it does not know it."],["p","He (who knows it) will keep his mouth shut and close the portals (of his nostrils). He will blunt his sharp points and unravel the complications of things; he will attemper his brightness, and bring himself into agreement with the obscurity (of others). This is called “the Mysterious Agreement.”"],["p","(Such an one) cannot be treated familiarly or distantly; he is beyond all consideration of profit or injury; of nobility or meanness:—he is the noblest man under heaven."]]},{"n":57,"b":[["p","A state may be ruled by (measures of) correction; weapons of war may be used with crafty dexterity; (but) the kingdom is made one’s own (only) by freedom from action and purpose."],["p","How do I know that it is so? By these facts:—In the kingdom the multiplication of prohibitive enactments increases the poverty of the people; the more implements to add to their profit that the people have, the greater disorder is there in the state and clan; the more acts of crafty dexterity that men possess, the more do strange contrivances appear; the more display there is of legislation, the more thieves and robbers there are."],["p","Therefore a sage has said, “I will do nothing (of purpose), and the people will be transformed of themselves; I will be fond of keeping still, and the people will of themselves become correct. I will take no trouble about it, and the people will of themselves become rich; I will manifest no ambition, and the people will of themselves attain to the primitive simplicity.”"]]},{"n":58,"b":[["v",["The government that seems the most unwise,","Oft goodness to the people best supplies;","That which is meddling, touching everything,","Will work but ill, and disappointment bring."]],["p","Misery!—happiness is to be found by its side! Happiness!—misery lurks beneath it! Who knows what either will come to in the end?"],["p","Shall we then dispense with correction? The (method of) correction shall by a turn become distortion, and the good in it shall by a turn become evil. The delusion of the people (on this point) has indeed subsisted for a long time."],["p","Therefore the sage is (like) a square which cuts no one (with its angles); (like) a corner which injures no one (with its sharpness). He is straightforward, but allows himself no license; he is bright, but does not dazzle."]]},{"n":59,"b":[["p","For regulating the human (in our constitution) and rendering the (proper) service to the heavenly, there is nothing like moderation."],["p","It is only by this moderation that there is effected an early return (to man’s normal state). That early return is what I call the repeated accumulation of the attributes (of the Tao). With that repeated accumulation of those attributes, there comes the subjugation (of every obstacle to such return). Of this subjugation we know not what shall be the limit; and when one knows not what the limit shall be, he may be the ruler of a state."],["p","He who possesses the mother of the state may continue long. His case is like that (of the plant) of which we say that its roots are deep and its flower stalks firm:—this is the way to secure that its enduring life shall long be seen."]]},{"n":60,"b":[["p","Governing a great state is like cooking small fish."],["p","Let the kingdom be governed according to the Tao, and the manes of the departed will not manifest their spiritual energy. It is not that those manes have not that spiritual energy, but it will not be employed to hurt men. It is not that it could not hurt men, but neither does the ruling sage hurt them."],["p","When these two do not injuriously affect each other, their good influences converge in the virtue (of the Tao)."]]},{"n":61,"b":[["p","What makes a great state is its being (like) a low-lying, down-flowing (stream);—it becomes the centre to which tend (all the small states) under heaven."],["p","(To illustrate from) the case of all females:—the female always overcomes the male by her stillness. Stillness may be considered (a sort of) abasement."],["p","Thus it is that a great state, by condescending to small states, gains them for itself; and that small states, by abasing themselves to a great state, win it over to them. In the one case the abasement leads to gaining adherents, in the other case to procuring favour."],["p","The great state only wishes to unite men together and nourish them; a small state only wishes to be received by, and to serve, the other. Each gets what it desires, but the great state must learn to abase itself."]]},{"n":62,"b":[["v",["Tao","No treasures give good men so rich a grace;","Bad men it guards, and doth their ill efface."]],["p","(Its) admirable words can purchase honour; (its) admirable deeds can raise their performer above others. Even men who are not good are not abandoned by it."],["p","Therefore when the sovereign occupies his place as the Son of Heaven, and he has appointed his three ducal ministers, though (a prince) were to send in a round symbol-of-rank large enough to fill both the hands, and that as the precursor of the team of horses (in the courtyard), such an offering would not be equal to (a lesson of) this Tao, which one might present on his knees."],["p","Why was it that the ancients prized this Tao so much? Was it not because it could be got by seeking for it, and the guilty could escape (from the stain of their guilt) by it? This is the reason why all under heaven consider it the most valuable thing."]]},{"n":63,"b":[["p","(It is the way of the Tao) to act without (thinking of) acting; to conduct affairs without (feeling the) trouble of them; to taste without discerning any flavour; to consider what is small as great, and a few as many; and to recompense injury with kindness."],["p","(The master of it) anticipates things that are difficult while they are easy, and does things that would become great while they are small. All difficult things in the world are sure to arise from a previous state in which they were easy, and all great things from one in which they were small. Therefore the sage, while he never does what is great, is able on that account to accomplish the greatest things."],["p","He who lightly promises is sure to keep but little faith; he who is continually thinking things easy is sure to find them difficult. Therefore the sage sees difficulty even in what seems easy, and so never has any difficulties."]]},{"n":64,"b":[["p","That which is at rest is easily kept hold of; before a thing has given indications of its presence, it is easy to take measures against it; that which is brittle is easily broken; that which is very small is easily dispersed. Action should be taken before a thing has made its appearance; order should be secured before disorder has begun."],["p","The tree which fills the arms grew from the tiniest sprout; the tower of nine storeys rose from a (small) heap of earth; the journey of a thousand li commenced with a single step."],["p","He who acts (with an ulterior purpose) does harm; he who takes hold of a thing (in the same way) loses his hold. The sage does not act (so), and therefore does no harm; he does not lay hold (so), and therefore does not lose his bold. (But) people in their conduct of affairs are constantly ruining them when they are on the eve of success. If they were careful at the end, as (they should be) at the beginning, they would not so ruin them."],["p","Therefore the sage desires what (other men) do not desire, and does not prize things difficult to get; he learns what (other men) do not learn, and turns back to what the multitude of men have passed by. Thus he helps the natural development of all things, and does not dare to act (with an ulterior purpose of his own)."]]},{"n":65,"b":[["p","The ancients who showed their skill in practising the Tao did so, not to enlighten the people, but rather to make them simple and ignorant."],["p","The difficulty in governing the people arises from their having much knowledge. He who (tries to) govern a state by his wisdom is a scourge to it; while he who does not (try to) do so is a blessing."],["p","He who knows these two things finds in them also his model and rule. Ability to know this model and rule constitutes what we call the mysterious excellence (of a governor). Deep and far-reaching is such mysterious excellence, showing indeed its possessor as opposite to others, but leading them to a great conformity to him."]]},{"n":66,"b":[["p","That whereby the rivers and seas are able to receive the homage and tribute of all the valley streams, is their skill in being lower than they;—it is thus that they are the kings of them all. So it is that the sage (ruler), wishing to be above men, puts himself by his words below them, and, wishing to be before them, places his person behind them."],["p","In this way though he has his place above them, men do not feel his weight, nor though he has his place before them, do they feel it an injury to them."],["p","Therefore all in the world delight to exalt him and do not weary of him. Because he does not strive, no one finds it possible to strive with him."]]},{"n":67,"b":[["p","All the world says that, while my Tao is great, it yet appears to be inferior (to other systems of teaching). Now it is just its greatness that makes it seem to be inferior. If it were like any other (system), for long would its smallness have been known!"],["p","But I have three precious things which I prize and hold fast. The first is gentleness; the second is economy; and the third is shrinking from taking precedence of others."],["p","With that gentleness I can be bold; with that economy I can be liberal; shrinking from taking precedence of others, I can become a vessel of the highest honour. Nowadays they give up gentleness and are all for being bold; economy, and are all for being liberal; the hindmost place, and seek only to be foremost;—(of all which the end is) death."],["p","Gentleness is sure to be victorious even in battle, and firmly to maintain its ground. Heaven will save its possessor, by his (very) gentleness protecting him."]]},{"n":68,"b":[["v",["He who in (Tao’s","He who fights with most good will","He who vanquishes yet still","He whose hests men most fulfil","Thus we say, “He ne’er contends,","Thus we say, “Men’s wills he bends,","Thus we say, “Like Heaven’s his ends,"]]]},{"n":69,"b":[["p","A master of the art of war has said, “I do not dare to be the host (to commence the war); I prefer to be the guest (to act on the defensive). I do not dare to advance an inch; I prefer to retire a foot.” This is called marshalling the ranks where there are no ranks; baring the arms (to fight) where there are no arms to bare; grasping the weapon where there is no weapon to grasp; advancing against the enemy where there is no enemy."],["p","There is no calamity greater than lightly engaging in war. To do that is near losing (the gentleness) which is so precious. Thus it is that when opposing weapons are (actually) crossed, he who deplores (the situation) conquers."]]},{"n":70,"b":[["p","My words are very easy to know, and very easy to practise; but there is no one in the world who is able to know and able to practise them."],["p","There is an originating and all-comprehending (principle) in my words, and an authoritative law for the things (which I enforce). It is because they do not know these, that men do not know me."],["p","They who know me are few, and I am on that account (the more) to be prized. It is thus that the sage wears (a poor garb of) hair cloth, while he carries his (signet of) jade in his bosom."]]},{"n":71,"b":[["p","To know and yet (think) we do not know is the highest (attainment); not to know (and yet think) we do know is a disease."],["p","It is simply by being pained at (the thought of) having this disease that we are preserved from it. The sage has not the disease. He knows the pain that would be inseparable from it, and therefore he does not have it."]]},{"n":72,"b":[["p","When the people do not fear what they ought to fear, that which is their great dread will come on them."],["p","Let them not thoughtlessly indulge themselves in their ordinary life; let them not act as if weary of what that life depends on."],["p","It is by avoiding such indulgence that such weariness does not arise."],["p","Therefore the sage knows (these things) of himself, but does not parade (his knowledge); loves, but does not (appear to set a) value on, himself. And thus he puts the latter alternative away and makes choice of the former."]]},{"n":73,"b":[["p","He whose boldness appears in his daring (to do wrong, in defiance of the laws) is put to death; he whose boldness appears in his not daring (to do so) lives on. Of these two cases the one appears to be advantageous, and the other to be injurious. But"],["v",["When Heaven’s anger smites a man,","Who the cause shall truly scan?"]],["p","On this account the sage feels a difficulty (as to what to do in the former case)."],["p","It is the way of Heaven not to strive, and yet it skilfully overcomes; not to speak, and yet it is skilful in (obtaining) a reply; does not call, and yet men come to it of themselves. Its demonstrations are quiet, and yet its plans are skilful and effective. The meshes of the net of Heaven are large; far apart, but letting nothing escape."]]},{"n":74,"b":[["p","The people do not fear death; to what purpose is it to (try to) frighten them with death? If the people were always in awe of death, and I could always seize those who do wrong, and put them to death, who would dare to do wrong?"],["p","There is always One who presides over the infliction death. He who would inflict death in the room of him who so presides over it may be described as hewing wood instead of a great carpenter. Seldom is it that he who undertakes the hewing, instead of the great carpenter, does not cut his own hands!"]]},{"n":75,"b":[["p","The people suffer from famine because of the multitude of taxes consumed by their superiors. It is through this that they suffer famine."],["p","The people are difficult to govern because of the (excessive) agency of their superiors (in governing them). It is through this that they are difficult to govern."],["p","The people make light of dying because of the greatness of their labours in seeking for the means of living. It is this which makes them think light of dying. Thus it is that to leave the subject of living altogether out of view is better than to set a high value on it."]]},{"n":76,"b":[["p","Man at his birth is supple and weak; at his death, firm and strong. (So it is with) all things. Trees and plants, in their early growth, are soft and brittle; at their death, dry and withered."],["p","Thus it is that firmness and strength are the concomitants of death; softness and weakness, the concomitants of life."],["p","Hence he who (relies on) the strength of his forces does not conquer; and a tree which is strong will fill the outstretched arms, (and thereby invites the feller.)"],["p","Therefore the place of what is firm and strong is below, and that of what is soft and weak is above."]]},{"n":77,"b":[["p","May not the Way (or Tao) of Heaven be compared to the (method of) bending a bow? The (part of the bow) which was high is brought low, and what was low is raised up. (So Heaven) diminishes where there is superabundance, and supplements where there is deficiency."],["p","It is the Way of Heaven to diminish superabundance, and to supplement deficiency. It is not so with the way of man. He takes away from those who have not enough to add to his own superabundance."],["p","Who can take his own superabundance and therewith serve all under heaven? Only he who is in possession of the Tao!"],["p","Therefore the (ruling) sage acts without claiming the results as his; he achieves his merit and does not rest (arrogantly) in it:—he does not wish to display his superiority."]]},{"n":78,"b":[["p","There is nothing in the world more soft and weak than water, and yet for attacking things that are firm and strong there is nothing that can take precedence of it;—for there is nothing (so effectual) for which it can be changed."],["p","Everyone in the world knows that the soft overcomes the hard, and the weak the strong, but no one is able to carry it out in practice."],["v",["Therefore a sage has said,","“He who accepts his state’s reproach,","To him who bears men’s direful woes"]],["p","Words that are strictly true seem to be paradoxical."]]},{"n":79,"b":[["p","When a reconciliation is effected (between two parties) after a great animosity, there is sure to be a grudge remaining (in the mind of the one who was wrong). And how can this be beneficial (to the other)?"],["p","Therefore (to guard against this), the sage keeps the left-hand portion of the record of the engagement, and does not insist on the (speedy) fulfilment of it by the other party. (So), he who has the attributes (of the Tao) regards (only) the conditions of the engagement, while he who has not those attributes regards only the conditions favourable to himself."],["p","In the Way of Heaven, there is no partiality of love; it is always on the side of the good man."]]},{"n":80,"b":[["p","In a little state with a small population, I would so order it, that, though there were individuals with the abilities of ten or a hundred men, there should be no employment of them; I would make the people, while looking on death as a grievous thing, yet not remove elsewhere (to avoid it)."],["p","Though they had boats and carriages, they should have no occasion to ride in them; though they had buff coats and sharp weapons, they should have no occasion to don or use them."],["p","I would make the people return to the use of knotted cords (instead of the written characters)."],["p","They should think their (coarse) food sweet; their (plain) clothes beautiful; their (poor) dwellings places of rest; and their common (simple) ways sources of enjoyment."],["p","There should be a neighbouring state within sight, and the voices of the fowls and dogs should be heard all the way from it to us, but I would make the people to old age, even to death, not have any intercourse with it."]]},{"n":81,"b":[["p","Sincere words are not fine; fine words are not sincere. Those who are skilled (in the Tao) do not dispute (about it); the disputatious are not skilled in it. Those who know (the Tao) are not extensively learned; the extensively learned do not know it."],["p","The sage does not accumulate (for himself). The more that he expends for others, the more does he possess of his own; the more that he gives to others, the more does he have himself."],["p","With all the sharpness of the Way of Heaven, it injures not; with all the doing in the way of the sage he does not strive."]]}],"own":[{"n":1,"b":[["s",["A way that can be put into words","is not the lasting Way.","A name that one can speak","is not the lasting Name."]],["s",["Before it has a name, it is the root of heaven and earth.","Once it is named, it is the mother of the ten thousand things."]],["s",["So be free of wanting, and you will see its mystery.","Keep wanting, and you will see only its edges."]],["s",["The two come from the same place and differ only in name.","Call that place the mystery,","mystery inside mystery:","the gate to everything wonderful."]]]},{"n":2,"b":[["s",["When everyone knows what beauty is, ugliness has appeared.","When everyone knows what goodness is, so has its opposite."]],["s",["Being and not-being produce one another.","Hard and easy complete one another.","Long and short are measured by one another.","High and low rest on one another.","Voice and music answer one another.","Before and after trail one another."]],["s",["So the sage works without forcing","and teaches without speeches.","The ten thousand things rise, and none is turned away.","They grow, and none is owned.","The sage acts and does not lean on the result,","finishes the work and does not stay to be praised.","Because nothing is claimed, nothing is lost."]]]},{"n":3,"b":[["s",["Do not exalt the talented,","and people will stop competing.","Do not prize rare goods,","and people will stop stealing.","Do not parade what is desirable,","and hearts will stay quiet."]],["s",["So the sage governs by emptying the heart of craving","and filling the belly,","by softening ambition","and strengthening the bones.","People are left without scheming and without longing,","and the clever ones do not dare to meddle."]],["s",["Act without forcing,","and nothing is left unordered."]]]},{"n":4,"b":[["s",["The Way is empty, and however you use it","you never find it full.","Deep as a well, it seems the ancestor of all things."]],["s",["It blunts what is sharp,","unties what is knotted,","dims the glare,","and settles in with the dust."]],["s",["Clear and quiet, it seems to stay.","I do not know whose child it is.","It looks older than any god."]]]},{"n":5,"b":[["s",["Heaven and earth are not sentimental.","They treat all things as straw dogs:","honored for the ceremony, then set aside.","The sage is not sentimental either,","and treats the people the same way."]],["s",["The space between heaven and earth works like a bellows:","empty, yet it never runs dry,","and the more it moves, the more it gives."]],["s",["Many words wear themselves out.","Better to keep to the center."]]]},{"n":6,"b":[["s",["The valley spirit never dies.","Call it the dark mother.","The gate of the dark mother","is the root of heaven and earth."]],["s",["It goes on and on, as if barely there,","and use it as you like, it is never used up."]]]},{"n":7,"b":[["s",["Heaven lasts and earth endures.","They last because they do not live for themselves,","and so they go on living."]],["s",["The sage stays behind, and finds himself in front;","sets the self aside, and the self is kept.","Is it not because there is no private aim","that private aims are fulfilled?"]]]},{"n":8,"b":[["s",["Goodness at its highest resembles water.","Water helps all things and does not compete.","It settles in the low places people avoid,","and so it is close to the Way."]],["s",["Choose the right ground to live on.","Keep the heart deep.","Be kind in giving.","Be true in speaking.","Be orderly in ruling.","Be capable in work.","Pick the right moment to move."]],["s",["Because it does not compete, it is never at fault."]]]},{"n":9,"b":[["s",["Fill a cup until it runs over.","Hone a blade too fine and it loses its edge.","A hall heaped with gold and jade cannot be guarded.","Pride in wealth and rank brings its own downfall."]],["s",["When the work is done, withdraw.","This is heaven’s way."]]]},{"n":10,"b":[["s",["Can you keep body and soul together","and never let them part?","Can you gather your breath until it is as soft as a baby’s?","Can you wash the dark mirror of the mind until it has no flaw?"]],["s",["Can you love the people and lead the country without forcing?","Can you open and close the gates of heaven","and still be like the mother bird?","Can you see in every direction and yet be unknowing?"]],["s",["It gives birth and it feeds.","It gives birth and does not keep.","It acts and does not boast.","It leads and does not rule.","This is called the dark virtue."]]]},{"n":11,"b":[["s",["Thirty spokes meet in one hub,","but the use of the wheel is in the hole at its center.","Clay is shaped into a jar,","but the use of the jar is in the hollow inside.","Doors and windows are cut to make a room,","but the use of the room is in the empty space."]],["s",["So what is there gives us profit,","and what is not there gives us use."]]]},{"n":12,"b":[["s",["Five colors make the eye blind.","Five notes make the ear deaf.","Five flavors dull the mouth.","Racing and hunting make the mind wild.","Treasures hard to get lead the feet astray."]],["s",["So the sage looks after the belly, not the eye:","takes the one and lets the other go."]]]},{"n":13,"b":[["s",["Praise and shame both shake us.","Honor and great trouble both come with having a body."]],["s",["Why do praise and shame both shake us?","Praise lifts you up, and from there you can only fall.","Getting it frightens you, and losing it frightens you."]],["s",["Why do honor and trouble come with the body?","My greatest trouble is that I have a self.","Without a self, what trouble could I have?"]],["s",["So whoever values the world as much as the self","can be trusted with the world,","and whoever loves the world as the self","can be handed its care."]]]},{"n":14,"b":[["s",["Look for it and you cannot see it: call it the colorless.","Listen for it and you cannot hear it: call it the soundless.","Reach for it and you cannot hold it: call it the formless.","These three cannot be pulled apart, and so they are one."]],["s",["Above, it is not bright. Below, it is not dark.","It goes on and on, cannot be named,","and goes back again into nothing.","It is the shape of the shapeless,","the image of no image.","Call it the blur, the glimmer."]],["s",["Meet it and you cannot see its face.","Follow it and you cannot see its back.","Hold to the ancient Way","to steer the present moment.","To know the beginning of things","is to find the thread of the Way."]]]},{"n":15,"b":[["s",["The old masters of the Way were subtle, dark and deep,","too deep to be understood.","Since they cannot be understood,","I can only describe how they looked:"]],["s",["Careful, like someone crossing a stream in winter.","Watchful, like someone wary of neighbors on every side.","Polite, like a guest.","Loose, like ice about to melt.","Plain, like uncarved wood.","Open, like a valley.","Cloudy, like muddy water."]],["s",["Who can make muddy water clear?","Stay still, and it clears itself.","Who can find rest?","Let things move on, and calm arises by itself."]],["s",["Those who hold to this Way do not wish to be full.","Because they are not full,","they can wear out and be made new again."]]]},{"n":16,"b":[["s",["Reach the utmost emptiness.","Hold to deep stillness.","The ten thousand things rise and fall,","and I watch them return."]],["s",["Plants flourish, and each goes back to its root.","Going back to the root is called stillness.","Stillness is called returning to what was given.","Returning is called the constant.","To know the constant is to be clear.","Not to know it is to stumble into trouble."]],["s",["Knowing the constant, you can hold everything.","Holding everything, you are fair.","Being fair, you are kingly.","Being kingly, you are like heaven.","Being like heaven, you are one with the Way.","One with the Way, you last,","and to the end of your life you are safe."]]]},{"n":17,"b":[["s",["The best rulers: the people barely know they exist.","Next come those they love and praise.","Next, those they fear.","Last, those they despise."]],["s",["Where trust is lacking, distrust follows.","The best ones speak little and weigh their words.","When the work is done and things have gone well,","the people say, “It came about by itself.”"]]]},{"n":18,"b":[["s",["When the great Way is lost, kindness and duty appear.","When cleverness arrives, great deceit arrives with it.","When families fall out, people start to praise dutiful children.","When the land sinks into disorder, people start to praise loyal ministers."]]]},{"n":19,"b":[["s",["Give up sainthood and throw away cleverness,","and the people will gain a hundredfold.","Give up preaching kindness and throw away rules of duty,","and love will come back to the family.","Give up scheming and throw away profit,","and there will be no thieves."]],["s",["These three sayings are not enough by themselves,","so add this:","show plainness, hold to the uncarved block,","want little, and set the self aside."]]]},{"n":20,"b":[["s",["Give up study and your worries end.","How far apart are “yes” and “yeah”?","How far apart are good and bad?","What others fear, I must fear too, and it never ends."]],["s",["Everyone else is bright and cheerful, as at a feast,","as if climbing a tower in spring.","I alone drift and show nothing,","like a baby who has not yet smiled,","weary, like someone with nowhere to go."]],["s",["Everyone has more than enough. I alone seem to have lost it all.","I have the mind of a fool, so blank and murky.","Others are sharp and shining. I alone am dim.","Others are clever and exact. I alone am vague,","tossed like the sea, blown like a wind that never lands."]],["s",["Everyone seems to have somewhere to go. I alone am stubborn and plain.","I differ from them in one thing only:","I prize being fed by the Mother."]]]},{"n":21,"b":[["s",["Great power takes its shape from the Way and from nothing else.","And the Way, as a thing, is dim and slippery.","Slippery and dim, yet there are images in it.","Dim and slippery, yet there are things in it.","Deep and dark, yet there is a seed in it,","and the seed is so real that it can be trusted."]],["s",["From the earliest days until now its name has never gone,","and by it we see how all things begin.","How do I know how all things begin?","By this."]]]},{"n":22,"b":[["s",["Bend, and you will be kept whole.","Curve, and you will be made straight.","Be hollow, and you will be filled.","Be worn, and you will be made new.","Have little, and you will gain.","Have much, and you will be confused."]],["s",["So the sage holds to the One and becomes a measure for the world.","Not showing himself, he shines.","Not justifying himself, he is respected.","Not boasting, he is given credit.","Not bragging, he lasts.","He does not compete, and so no one can compete with him."]],["s",["The old saying, “Bend, and you will be kept whole,”","is no empty phrase.","Be truly whole, and everything comes back to you."]]]},{"n":23,"b":[["s",["Few words is the way of nature.","A gale does not blow all morning.","A downpour does not last all day.","Who makes them? Heaven and earth.","If even heaven and earth cannot make them last,","how much less can people?"]],["s",["So whoever works with the Way becomes one with the Way.","Whoever works with virtue becomes one with virtue.","Whoever works with loss becomes one with loss.","The Way is glad to receive those who join it.","Virtue is glad to receive those who join it.","Loss is glad to receive those who join it."]],["s",["Where trust is lacking, distrust follows."]]]},{"n":24,"b":[["s",["Stand on tiptoe and you cannot stand firm.","Take giant strides and you cannot walk far.","Show yourself off and you do not shine.","Justify yourself and you are not respected.","Boast and you get no credit.","Brag and you do not last."]],["s",["To the Way, these are leftover scraps and useless growths.","Everyone dislikes them,","so those who follow the Way do not stay among them."]]]},{"n":25,"b":[["s",["Something was formed out of the formless,","born before heaven and earth.","Silent. Empty. Standing alone and never changing.","Circling everywhere and never tiring.","It could be the mother of the world."]],["s",["I do not know its name.","I give it the word “Way.”","If I must say more, I say “great.”","Being great, it goes forth.","Going forth, it reaches far.","Reaching far, it comes back."]],["s",["Great is the Way. Great is heaven. Great is earth.","Great, too, is the person.","There are four great things in the realm,","and a person is counted among them."]],["s",["People follow the earth.","Earth follows heaven.","Heaven follows the Way.","The Way follows what is so of itself."]]]},{"n":26,"b":[["s",["Weight is the root of lightness.","Stillness is the lord of haste."]],["s",["So a wise traveler, all day long,","never lets go of the loaded wagon.","Though marvelous sights go by,","he stays at ease and unmoved."]],["s",["How then can the ruler of ten thousand chariots","carry himself lightly before the world?","Be light, and you lose your root.","Be restless, and you lose your command."]]]},{"n":27,"b":[["s",["A good traveler leaves no track.","A good speaker leaves no fault to be found.","A good counter needs no tally sticks.","A good door has no bolt, yet it cannot be opened.","A good knot uses no rope, yet it cannot be untied."]],["s",["So the sage is always good at helping people, and no one is cast away.","Always good at using things, and nothing is wasted.","This is called following the light."]],["s",["The good person is the teacher of the one who is not good.","The one who is not good is the good person’s raw material.","Not to honor the teacher, not to care for the material,","is to be lost, however clever you are.","This is called the deep secret."]]]},{"n":28,"b":[["s",["Know what is male, but keep to what is female,","and be a valley for the whole world.","Being a valley, you never lose your lasting power,","and you return to being a child."]],["s",["Know the bright, but keep to the dark,","and be a model for the world.","Being a model, you never stray from your lasting power,","and you return to the limitless."]],["s",["Know honor, but keep to humble ground,","and be a valley for the whole world.","Being a valley, your lasting power is full,","and you return to the uncarved wood."]],["s",["When the wood is cut, it becomes tools.","The sage uses the tools to lead,","yet the greatest carving does not cut."]]]},{"n":29,"b":[["s",["Whoever wants to take the world and shape it to their will,","I have seen that they do not succeed.","The world is a sacred vessel.","It cannot be worked on like a lump of clay.","Work on it and you spoil it.","Grasp it and you lose it."]],["s",["Some things lead and some things follow.","Some breathe warm and some breathe cold.","Some are strong and some are weak.","Some are lifted up and some are cast down."]],["s",["So the sage lets go of too much,","lets go of too grand,","lets go of too far."]]]},{"n":30,"b":[["s",["Whoever advises a ruler in the way of the Way","does not use weapons to bully the world.","Such things come back around."]],["s",["Where armies have camped, thorns and brambles grow.","After a great war, bad years follow."]],["s",["A good commander gets the result and stops.","He does not dare to push on and take more by force.","He gets the result and does not boast.","He gets the result and does not brag.","He gets the result and is not proud.","He gets the result because he had no choice,","and he gets it without using force."]],["s",["Whatever reaches its prime then grows old.","That is going against the Way,","and what goes against the Way ends early."]]]},{"n":31,"b":[["s",["Weapons, even fine ones, are tools of misfortune.","People shrink from them,","so one who follows the Way keeps away from them."]],["s",["At home, a gentleman honors the left, the side of good fortune.","In war he honors the right, the side of ill fortune.","Weapons are tools of misfortune, not a gentleman’s tools.","When he has no choice but to use them,","he does it with a calm, quiet heart,","and he takes no pleasure in winning.","To take pleasure in it is to take pleasure in killing,","and one who delights in killing","will never get what he wants from the world."]],["s",["On happy days the left is honored.","On sad days the right is honored.","In the army the second-in-command stands on the left","and the commander on the right,","which means they keep to the rules of a funeral."]],["s",["When so many have been killed, we should weep for them.","A victory in battle should be handled like a funeral."]]]},{"n":32,"b":[["s",["The Way is forever without a name. It is the uncarved block.","Small as it is, no one in the world can master it.","If rulers could keep to it, all things would come of their own accord.","Heaven and earth would join and let the sweet dew fall,","and people, with no one commanding them, would be fair to one another."]],["s",["Once the block is cut, there are names.","Once there are names, one must learn where to stop.","Knowing where to stop, one stays out of danger."]],["s",["The Way in the world is like a stream running down a valley","into rivers and the sea."]]]},{"n":33,"b":[["s",["To know others is wisdom.","To know yourself is clear sight.","To overcome others takes force.","To overcome yourself takes strength.","To know when you have enough is to be rich.","To keep on going is to have a will.","To stay where you belong is to last.","To die and not be lost is to live long."]]]},{"n":34,"b":[["s",["The great Way flows everywhere, to the left and to the right.","All things depend on it for life, and it turns none away.","It finishes its work and claims no title.","It clothes and feeds all things and does not rule them."]],["s",["Always without wanting, it may be named small.","All things return to it, and it does not rule them:","it may be named great.","Because it never sets out to be great,","it is able to become great."]]]},{"n":35,"b":[["s",["Hold to the great image and the whole world will come to you.","They come and are not hurt.","They find peace, safety and ease."]],["s",["Music and good food make travelers stop.","But the Way, when it is put into words, is thin and has no taste.","Look at it: there is not enough to see.","Listen to it: there is not enough to hear.","Use it: you will never use it up."]]]},{"n":36,"b":[["s",["What is to be shrunk must first be stretched.","What is to be weakened must first be made strong.","What is to be thrown down must first be raised up.","What is to be taken must first be given.","This is called seeing the hidden light."]],["s",["The soft and weak overcome the hard and strong.","A fish should not leave the deep water.","A country’s sharpest tools should not be shown to the people."]]]},{"n":37,"b":[["s",["The Way never acts, and yet nothing is left undone.","If rulers could hold to it, all things would change by themselves.","If, in changing, they began to want and grasp,","I would settle them with the nameless uncarved block.","The nameless block brings freedom from wanting.","Without wanting there is quiet,","and the world puts itself in order."]]]},{"n":38,"b":[["s",["The highest virtue is not aware of being virtuous, and so it is virtue.","The lowest virtue never lets go of being virtuous, and so it is not.","The highest virtue does nothing and has no aim.","The lowest virtue is always doing and always has an aim.","The highest kindness acts and has no aim.","The highest justice acts and has an aim.","The highest courtesy acts, and when no one answers,","it bares its arm and hauls people along."]],["s",["So when the Way is lost, there is virtue.","When virtue is lost, there is kindness.","When kindness is lost, there is justice.","When justice is lost, there is ceremony.","Ceremony is the thin edge of loyalty and good faith,","and the start of disorder.","Knowing things in advance is the flower of the Way","and the beginning of foolishness."]],["s",["So the great person stays with what is solid and not what is thin,","in what ripens and not in what blooms.","He lets go of that and takes this."]]]},{"n":39,"b":[["s",["Of old these obtained the One:","Heaven, which by the One became clear.","Earth, which by the One became steady.","The spirits, which by the One became powerful.","The valley, which by the One became full.","The ten thousand things, which by the One came alive.","Rulers, who by the One set the world right.","All of them are what they are because of the One."]],["s",["Without its clearness, heaven would crack.","Without its steadiness, earth would give way.","Without their power, the spirits would fade.","Without its fullness, the valley would run dry.","Without life, the ten thousand things would die out.","Without respect, the rulers would fall."]],["s",["So the noble has the humble for a root,","and the high has the low for a foundation.","That is why rulers call themselves orphan, lonely one, unworthy.","Is that not taking the humble as the root? Is it not?"]],["s",["Take a carriage apart and there is no carriage left to praise.","So do not wish to ring like jade, or to clatter like stone."]]]},{"n":40,"b":[["s",["Returning is how the Way moves.","Yielding is how the Way works.","All things under heaven are born from being,","and being is born from not-being."]]]},{"n":41,"b":[["s",["When the best students hear of the Way, they set out to follow it with all their effort.","When ordinary students hear of the Way, they keep it for a while and lose it for a while.","When the worst students hear of the Way, they laugh out loud.","If they did not laugh, it would not be the Way."]],["s",["So the old sayings put it like this:","The bright path looks dark.","The way forward looks like going back.","The smooth road looks rough.","High virtue looks like an empty valley.","Great purity looks stained.","Wide virtue looks as if it lacks something.","Steady virtue looks lazy.","What is truly solid looks as if it might melt."]],["s",["The great square has no corners.","The great vessel is the last to be finished.","The great sound is hard to hear.","The great image has no shape."]],["s",["The Way hides and has no name,","and yet it alone is good at lending to all things and bringing them to completion."]]]},{"n":42,"b":[["s",["From the Way came one.","From one came two.","From two came three.","From three came the ten thousand things."]],["s",["All things carry the dark on their backs and hold the light in their arms,","and their breath mixes the two into harmony."]],["s",["People hate to be called orphan, lonely one, unworthy,","yet rulers use these names for themselves.","So it is with things:","lose, and you gain; gain, and you lose."]],["s",["What others teach, I also teach:","“The violent do not die a natural death.”","I take this as the root of my teaching."]]]},{"n":43,"b":[["s",["The softest thing under heaven overruns the hardest.","That which has no form slips in where there is no crack.","From this I learn how much good lies in not striving."]],["s",["Teaching without speaking, and gain without striving:","almost nothing in the world can compare with them."]]]},{"n":44,"b":[["s",["Your name or your body: which is closer to you?","Your body or your wealth: which counts for more?","Gaining or losing: which does you harm?"]],["s",["So whoever loves too much pays too much.","Whoever stores up too much loses too much.","Know when you have enough, and you are not shamed.","Know when to stop, and you are not in danger.","Then you can last."]]]},{"n":45,"b":[["s",["What is most complete seems to be lacking, and yet it never wears out.","What is most full seems to be empty, and yet it never runs dry.","The straightest seems bent.","The most skilled seems clumsy.","The best speaker seems to stammer."]],["s",["Moving fast beats the cold.","Standing still beats the heat.","Be clear and still, and you set the world right."]]]},{"n":46,"b":[["s",["When the world follows the Way,","swift horses are sent back to pull the dung cart.","When the world has lost the Way,","war horses are bred on the border."]],["s",["There is no crime worse than giving way to desire.","There is no disaster worse than not knowing when you have enough.","There is no fault worse than the itch to get more.","So to know that enough is enough","is to have enough, always."]]]},{"n":47,"b":[["s",["The world can be known without a step out of the door,","and heaven’s Way can be seen without a glance through the window.","The farther a person travels, the less that person knows."]],["s",["So the sage arrives without going,","names without seeing,","and completes without doing."]]]},{"n":48,"b":[["s",["Study, and each day you gather something more.","Follow the Way, and each day you let something go.","You let go, and let go again, until you arrive at not-doing.","Do not do, and there is nothing left undone."]],["s",["To win the world, always let things be.","Once you start fussing, you are not fit to win it."]]]},{"n":49,"b":[["s",["The sage has no fixed mind of his own.","He takes the people’s mind as his mind."]],["s",["To those who are good, I am good.","To those who are not good, I am also good,","and so goodness is gained.","To those who are honest, I am honest.","To those who are not honest, I am also honest,","and so honesty is gained."]],["s",["The sage lives in the world with care,","and for the world he blends his mind with the minds of others.","People all fix their eyes and ears on him,","and he treats them all as his children."]]]},{"n":50,"b":[["s",["We come out into life and go back into death.","Out of every ten people, three are companions of life,","three are companions of death,","and three more are headed from life toward death simply by living too hard.","Why is this? Because they cling too tightly to life."]],["s",["I have heard that one who knows how to look after life","walks the hills without meeting a rhinoceros or a tiger,","and goes into battle without armor or weapons.","The rhinoceros finds nowhere to stick its horn.","The tiger finds nowhere to sink its claws.","The soldier finds nowhere to thrust his blade.","Why? Because in such a person there is no place for death to enter."]]]},{"n":51,"b":[["s",["The Way gives birth to things.","Virtue feeds them.","Matter shapes them.","Circumstance completes them."]],["s",["So all things honor the Way and prize virtue.","No one commands this respect.","It is always given freely."]],["s",["The Way gives birth, and virtue feeds.","They raise things up, rear them, shelter them, and bring them to ripeness.","They give birth and do not own.","They act and do not depend on it.","They lead and do not rule.","This is called the dark virtue."]]]},{"n":52,"b":[["s",["The world had a beginning, and that beginning is the mother of the world.","When you know the mother, you can know her children.","When you know the children, hold on to the mother again,","and to the end of your life you are safe."]],["s",["Stop up the openings and close the door,","and your life will be free of toil.","Open the openings and busy yourself with affairs,","and your life will be beyond help."]],["s",["To see the small is called clarity.","To keep to the soft is called strength.","Use the light, then return to its clear source,","and you will bring no harm on yourself.","This is called following the constant."]]]},{"n":53,"b":[["s",["If I had even a small bit of sense,","I would walk the great road and fear only to leave it.","The great road is smooth and level, yet people love the side paths."]],["s",["The palace is kept spotless, while the fields run to weeds","and the granaries stand empty.","Yet they wear fine clothes and carry sharp swords,","they stuff themselves with food and drink,","and they have wealth to spare.","This is called the pride of thieves.","It is far from the Way."]]]},{"n":54,"b":[["s",["What is well planted cannot be pulled up.","What is well held cannot be taken away.","Children and grandchildren will honor such a person without end."]],["s",["Cultivate it in yourself, and your virtue becomes real.","Cultivate it in the family, and your virtue overflows.","Cultivate it in the village, and your virtue lasts.","Cultivate it in the country, and your virtue grows rich.","Cultivate it in the world, and your virtue is everywhere."]],["s",["So look at a person through the person,","a family through the family,","a village through the village,","a country through the country,","the world through the world.","How do I know that this is how the world is?","By this."]]]},{"n":55,"b":[["s",["One who is rich in virtue resembles a baby just born.","Wasps and scorpions and snakes do not sting it.","Wild beasts do not seize it.","Birds of prey do not strike it.","The bones are weak and the muscles tender, yet the grip is strong.","It does not yet know about man and woman, yet its body stirs:","that is the fullness of life-force.","It can cry all day and not grow hoarse:","that is the fullness of harmony."]],["s",["To know harmony is to know the constant.","To know the constant is to be clear.","To push life along is a bad omen.","For the mind to drive the breath is to use force.","What grows strong then grows old.","That is going against the Way,","and what goes against the Way ends early."]]]},{"n":56,"b":[["s",["Those who know do not talk.","Those who talk do not know."]],["s",["Stop up the openings. Close the door.","Blunt what is sharp. Untie what is knotted.","Dim the glare. Settle in with the dust.","This is called the dark sameness."]],["s",["You cannot draw such a person close, and you cannot push them away.","You cannot help them, and you cannot harm them.","You cannot raise them up, and you cannot bring them low.","And so they are the most honored thing under heaven."]]]},{"n":57,"b":[["s",["Rule a country by being straight.","Fight a war by using surprise.","Win the world by leaving things alone.","How do I know this works? Like this:"]],["s",["The more rules and bans there are, the poorer people become.","The more sharp weapons people have, the more the country is troubled.","The more cunning people grow, the more strange things appear.","The more laws are posted, the more thieves there are."]],["s",["So the sage says:","I do nothing, and people change by themselves.","I love stillness, and people put themselves right.","I make no work, and people grow rich.","I want nothing, and people become plain and simple."]]]},{"n":58,"b":[["s",["When the government is quiet and slow, the people are simple and warm.","When the government is sharp and prying, the people are sly and torn."]],["s",["Misfortune is what good fortune leans on.","Good fortune is what misfortune hides inside.","Who can say where it ends?","There is no fixed rule.","What is right turns into the strange,","and what is good turns into the sinister.","People have been confused for a very long time."]],["s",["So the sage is square but does not cut,","has edges but does not wound,","is straight but does not stretch,","shines but does not dazzle."]]]},{"n":59,"b":[["s",["In ruling people and serving heaven, nothing is better than keeping to the measure.","Only by keeping to the measure can you return early to the Way.","To return early is to pile up virtue.","Piled-up virtue can overcome anything.","When nothing is beyond you, no one can see your limit.","When no one can see your limit, you can hold a country.","Hold the mother of the country and you can last."]],["s",["This is called deep roots and a firm stem,","the way to a long life and a far view."]]]},{"n":60,"b":[["s",["Governing a great country is like cooking a small fish.","Rule the world with the Way, and the ghosts have no power.","It is not that the ghosts have no power,","but that their power does not hurt people.","It is not only that their power does not hurt people:","the sage does not hurt people either.","Since neither does harm, their virtue joins and returns to the people."]]]},{"n":61,"b":[["s",["A great country is like low ground where rivers meet,","the place where the whole world gathers,","the female of the world.","The female overcomes the male by being still,","and being still, she lies low."]],["s",["So a great country that lowers itself before a small one wins the small one over.","A small country that lowers itself before a great one is taken in by it.","Some lower themselves in order to win, and some lower themselves and are taken in."]],["s",["A great country only wants to gather people up and look after them.","A small country only wants to join in and be of use.","Each gets what it wants,","and the great one ought to be the one that lowers itself."]]]},{"n":62,"b":[["s",["The Way is the hidden storehouse of all things.","It is the treasure of the good, and the shelter of those who are not good."]],["s",["Beautiful words can be sold in the market.","Fine deeds can be offered as gifts.","So why throw away anyone who is not good?"]],["s",["When a new ruler is crowned and his three ministers are named,","better than presenting jade discs, and horses with their chariots,","is to sit still and offer this Way."]],["s",["Why did the ancients prize this Way so much?","Did they not say that those who seek it find it,","and those who have done wrong are forgiven by it?","That is why the world holds it dearest."]]]},{"n":63,"b":[["s",["Act without acting.","Work without fuss.","Taste what has no taste.","Count the small as great and the little as much.","Answer a wrong with kindness."]],["s",["Deal with the hard while it is yet easy.","Do the great while it is yet small.","The hardest things in the world begin as easy things.","The greatest begin as small.","So the sage never sets out to do something great,","and that is how he achieves it."]],["s",["One who promises lightly is rarely trusted.","One who finds everything easy meets nothing but difficulty.","So the sage treats even the simple as hard,","and in the end meets no difficulty at all."]]]},{"n":64,"b":[["s",["What is calm is easy to hold.","What has not yet shown itself is easy to plan for.","The fragile breaks easily.","The slight is easily blown apart.","Deal with a thing before it exists.","Bring order before the disorder comes."]],["s",["A tree you cannot reach around grows from a hair-thin shoot.","A tower nine stories high starts from a basket of earth.","A road of a thousand miles is begun beneath your foot."]],["s",["Whoever acts, spoils.","Whoever grasps, loses.","So the sage does not act, and so does not spoil.","He does not grasp, and so does not lose."]],["s",["People often fail just when they are about to succeed.","Be as watchful at the finish as at the start,","and you will not fail."]],["s",["So the sage wants what is not wanted","and does not prize hard-to-get goods.","He learns what is not learned,","and goes back to what everyone else has passed by.","He helps all things become what they are,","and does not dare to force them."]]]},{"n":65,"b":[["s",["Those who were good at following the Way in ancient times","did not use it to make people clever.","They used it to keep people simple."]],["s",["People are hard to govern when they know too much.","To rule a country by cleverness is to rob the country.","To rule it without cleverness is to bless the country."]],["s",["Know these two, and you have a pattern.","Always to know the pattern is called the dark virtue.","The dark virtue is deep and far-reaching.","It turns back with all things,","and then brings the great harmony."]]]},{"n":66,"b":[["s",["Rivers and seas rule the hundred valleys","because they are good at lying lower than them.","That is how they rule."]],["s",["So a sage who wants to be over the people","must speak humbly to them.","One who wants to lead them","must put himself behind them."]],["s",["So when the sage is above, the people do not feel the weight.","When he is in front, the people do not feel hurt.","The whole world is glad to push him forward and does not tire of him.","Because he does not compete,","no one in the world can compete with him."]]]},{"n":67,"b":[["s",["Everyone says my Way is great, but that it looks like nothing else.","It is only because it is great that it looks like nothing else.","If it looked like something, it would long ago have become small."]],["s",["I have three treasures that I hold and guard.","The first is mercy.","The second is thrift.","The third is not daring to put myself ahead of others."]],["s",["Being merciful, I can be brave.","Being thrifty, I can be generous.","Not daring to put myself first, I can grow into a leader."]],["s",["People today throw away mercy and want courage.","They throw away thrift and want to be generous.","They throw away the rear and want to be in front.","That is death."]],["s",["Mercy wins the battle it fights and holds the ground it defends.","Heaven will save and guard the one it wishes to keep, by means of mercy."]]]},{"n":68,"b":[["s",["A good soldier is not warlike.","A good fighter is not angry.","A good winner does not take on the enemy directly.","A good employer of people takes the lower place."]],["s",["This is called the virtue of not competing.","This is called using other people’s strength.","This is called being a match for heaven, the highest rule of old."]]]},{"n":69,"b":[["s",["The strategists have a saying:","“I do not dare to be the host. I would rather be the guest.","I do not dare to advance an inch. I would rather retreat a foot.”","This is called marching without marching,","rolling up your sleeves with no arm to show,","holding on with no weapon,","and facing an enemy who is not there."]],["s",["There is no greater disaster than to think lightly of the enemy.","To think lightly of the enemy is to lose my treasures.","So when two armies meet on equal terms,","the one that grieves is the one that wins."]]]},{"n":70,"b":[["s",["What I say is simple to grasp and simple to carry out,","and still no one under heaven grasps it or carries it out."]],["s",["My words have an ancestor.","My actions have a master.","Because people do not know this, they do not know me.","Those who know me are few,","and that is why I am so prized.","So the sage wears rough cloth and carries jade in his heart."]]]},{"n":71,"b":[["s",["To know that you do not know is the best.","To not know, and to think that you know, is an illness.","Only by being sick of the illness can you be free of it.","The sage is not ill because he is sick of the illness,","and so he is not ill."]]]},{"n":72,"b":[["s",["When people no longer fear authority, a greater authority is on its way.","Do not crowd their homes.","Do not wear out their way of life.","If you do not wear them out, they will not grow weary of you."]],["s",["So the sage knows himself, but does not show himself off.","He cares for himself, but does not set himself above others.","He lets go of that and takes this."]]]},{"n":73,"b":[["s",["Brave in daring, you are killed.","Brave in not daring, you live.","Of these two, one does good and one does harm.","Who knows why heaven dislikes what it dislikes?","Even the sage finds this hard to say."]],["s",["The way of heaven does not compete, yet it wins.","It does not speak, yet it answers.","It does not call, yet things come to it.","It is unhurried, yet its plans are sound.","Heaven’s net is wide, wide.","Its mesh is coarse, yet nothing slips through."]]]},{"n":74,"b":[["s",["If people do not fear death, why frighten them with death?","Suppose people always feared death,","and we caught those who do strange things and put them to death:","who would dare?"]],["s",["There is always a Killer whose task is to kill.","To kill in the Killer’s place","is like cutting wood in the master carpenter’s stead.","Seldom does anyone who cuts in the carpenter’s stead","escape without wounding their own hands."]]]},{"n":75,"b":[["s",["People go hungry because those above them eat up too much in taxes.","That is why they go hungry.","People are hard to rule because those above them interfere.","That is why they are hard to rule.","People make light of death because those above them reach for a rich life.","That is why they make light of death."]],["s",["Only one who does not strain after life","is wiser than one who prizes life."]]]},{"n":76,"b":[["s",["A person is born soft and weak, and dies hard and stiff.","The ten thousand things, the grasses and the trees,","are soft and tender when alive, dry and brittle when dead.","So the hard and stiff are companions of death,","and the soft and weak are companions of life."]],["s",["An army that is strong will not win.","A tree that is strong will be cut down.","The strong and the great take the low place.","The soft and the weak take the high place."]]]},{"n":77,"b":[["s",["The way of heaven is like the stringing of a bow:","the high is pulled down and the low is raised up.","What is too much is reduced, and what is too little is made up.","The way of heaven takes from those who have too much","and gives to those who do not have enough.","The way of people is not so.","It takes from those who do not have enough","and gives to those who already have too much."]],["s",["Who can take what he has in excess and offer it to the world?","Only one who has the Way.","So the sage acts and does not depend on it,","completes his work and does not stay,","and does not wish to show how good he is."]]]},{"n":78,"b":[["s",["Nothing under heaven is more yielding than water,","yet for wearing down what is hard and strong, nothing surpasses it.","There is nothing to put in its place.","The weak beats the strong.","The gentle beats the hard.","All the world knows it, and none can practice it."]],["s",["So the sage says:","Whoever takes on the disgrace of the country is called the master of its altars.","Whoever takes on the misfortunes of the country is called king of the world.","True words seem the opposite of what they mean."]]]},{"n":79,"b":[["s",["When a great quarrel is patched up, some bitterness is sure to remain.","How can that be called good?"]],["s",["So the sage keeps his half of the contract","and does not press others for payment.","One with virtue looks after the contract.","One without virtue looks after the collecting.","The way of heaven has no favorites.","It always stays with the good person."]]]},{"n":80,"b":[["s",["A small country with few people.","Let it have tools that do the work of ten or a hundred, and let them go unused.","Let the people take death seriously, and not wander far from home.","Though they have boats and carts, they have no need to ride them.","Though they have armor and weapons, they have no reason to show them.","Let them go back to tying knots for keeping records."]],["s",["They enjoy their food.","They are pleased with their clothes.","They are at ease in their homes.","They love their own ways.","A neighboring country lies close enough to be seen,","close enough that the crowing of its cocks and the barking of its dogs can be heard,","yet people grow old and die without ever going there."]]]},{"n":81,"b":[["s",["True words are not beautiful.","Beautiful words are not true.","The good do not argue.","Those who argue are not good.","Those who know are not learned.","The learned do not know."]],["s",["The sage does not store things up.","Having done all he can for others, he has more than before.","Having given all he has to others, he is richer still.","The way of heaven helps and does not harm.","The way of the sage is to act and not to compete."]]]}]};
